A distinct, smaller quic_transport_parameters value for the ClientHelloOuter
of a split Encrypted Client Hello, independent of the ClientHelloInner's
(the real one, unaffected). BoringSSL always sent the same bytes to both:
Chrome/quiche never asks for anything else (captured: Chrome's outer hello
over QUIC carries the full, ordinary parameter set), but Firefox's neqo
does not — its outer is only ever a fallback the server completes if it
rejects the real, encrypted hello, so it forwards a small allowlisted
subset (`neqo_transport::tparams::TransportParameters::filter_ch_outer`) —
for a stock client, exactly three ever hold a value:
`max_ack_delay`, `initial_src_cid`, `version_information`, in ascending
transport parameter ID order.

- SSL_set_quic_transport_params_outer: configure the reduced outer value.
  Unset (the default): unchanged behaviour, the same bytes cover the outer
  hello too, compressible as before.

Once configured, the extension is written directly for both hello types,
never through the "compressible" path: ECH's compression assumes an
extension is byte-for-byte identical between ClientHelloOuter and Inner,
and has the server reconstruct EncodedClientHelloInner (the encrypted,
AAD-bound one) by copying the *outer's* actual bytes for it. With a
deliberately different, reduced outer value, that reconstruction would use
the wrong bytes and fail ECH's confirmation check on the client.

The legacy QUIC extension codepoint (0xffa5) is not covered — no captured
client pairs it with ECH, so a caller using both keeps the old, full,
compressible behaviour instead of a half-modelled split.

diff --git a/include/openssl/ssl.h b/include/openssl/ssl.h
index a140e34..ac08701 100644
--- a/include/openssl/ssl.h
+++ b/include/openssl/ssl.h
@@ -4172,6 +4172,25 @@ OPENSSL_EXPORT int SSL_set_quic_transport_params(SSL *ssl,
 OPENSSL_EXPORT void SSL_get_peer_quic_transport_params(
     const SSL *ssl, const uint8_t **out_params, size_t *out_params_len);
 
+// SSL_set_quic_transport_params_outer configures a distinct
+// quic_transport_parameters extension value that a client sends only in the
+// ClientHelloOuter of a split Encrypted Client Hello (draft-ietf-tls-esni),
+// instead of the value configured with |SSL_set_quic_transport_params|
+// (which keeps covering the ClientHelloInner, and every ClientHello when ECH
+// is not in use). Some QUIC stacks send a reduced parameter set in the
+// outer hello, since it is only ever a fallback the server completes if it
+// rejects the real, encrypted one, so most parameters would be both
+// meaningless there and a source of real, distinguishing values a hello
+// otherwise meant to look generic should not carry. |params| only needs to
+// be valid for the duration of this call. Has no effect unless ECH sends a
+// split ClientHelloOuter/Inner or |SSL_set_quic_use_legacy_codepoint|
+// selects the legacy codepoint, in which cases the value from
+// |SSL_set_quic_transport_params| covers both hellos, as if this had not
+// been called. Returns 1 on success, 0 on failure.
+OPENSSL_EXPORT int SSL_set_quic_transport_params_outer(SSL *ssl,
+                                                       const uint8_t *params,
+                                                       size_t params_len);
+
 // SSL_set_quic_use_legacy_codepoint configures whether to use the legacy QUIC
 // extension codepoint 0xffa5 as opposed to the official value 57. Call with
 // |use_legacy| set to 1 to use 0xffa5 and call with 0 to use 57. By default,
diff --git a/ssl/extensions.cc b/ssl/extensions.cc
index 99281ff..0a22b84 100644
--- a/ssl/extensions.cc
+++ b/ssl/extensions.cc
@@ -2925,6 +2925,40 @@ static bool ext_quic_transport_params_add_clienthello_impl(
 static bool ext_quic_transport_params_add_clienthello(
     const SSL_HANDSHAKE *hs, CBB *out, CBB *out_compressible,
     ssl_client_hello_type_t type) {
+  // |quic_transport_params_outer|, when configured, makes the
+  // ClientHelloOuter's quic_transport_parameters different from the
+  // ClientHelloInner's. Such an extension must never go through
+  // |out_compressible|, on *either* hello: ECH's compression assumes an
+  // extension written there while building ClientHelloInner is
+  // byte-for-byte what the already-sent ClientHelloOuter also carries, and
+  // has the server reconstruct the encrypted, AAD-bound
+  // EncodedClientHelloInner by copying the *outer's* actual bytes for it —
+  // which here are the deliberately different, reduced ones, corrupting
+  // that reconstruction and failing ECH's confirmation check on the
+  // client. So, like server_name's public-name override, this writes
+  // directly to |out| for both hello types once configured. See
+  // |SSL_set_quic_transport_params_outer|.
+  if (!hs->config->quic_transport_params_outer.empty() &&
+      !hs->config->quic_use_legacy_codepoint) {
+    // Same misconfiguration checks as the non-outer path below, against the
+    // base value: the outer one is never sent on its own, without the real
+    // ClientHelloInner value also having been configured.
+    if (hs->config->quic_transport_params.empty() && !SSL_is_quic(hs->ssl)) {
+      return true;
+    }
+    if (hs->config->quic_transport_params.empty() || !SSL_is_quic(hs->ssl)) {
+      OPENSSL_PUT_ERROR(SSL, SSL_R_QUIC_TRANSPORT_PARAMETERS_MISCONFIGURED);
+      return false;
+    }
+    const Array<uint8_t> &params = type == ssl_client_hello_outer
+                                        ? hs->config->quic_transport_params_outer
+                                        : hs->config->quic_transport_params;
+    CBB contents;
+    return CBB_add_u16(out, TLSEXT_TYPE_quic_transport_parameters) &&
+           CBB_add_u16_length_prefixed(out, &contents) &&
+           CBB_add_bytes(&contents, params.data(), params.size()) &&
+           CBB_flush(out);
+  }
   return ext_quic_transport_params_add_clienthello_impl(
       hs, out_compressible, /*use_legacy_codepoint=*/false);
 }
